+\paragraph{Bloch's theorem.}
+We use Bloch's theorem \cite{Bloch} in the formulation of
+\cite[Thm.~6]{LalinRam}, in the normalizations of
+\cite[Def.~5, eq.~(10)]{LalinRam} (which coincide verbatim with our series
+implementation of $D_E$, checked to $60$ digits on both curves): for
+$f,g\in\Q(E)$ with $\{f,g\}\in K_2(E)$,
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:bloch}
+  \int_{\bar\gamma}\eta(f,g)
+  =D_E\big((f)\diamond(g)\big),
+\end{equation}
+where $\bar\gamma$ is a generator of the coinvariant quotient
+$H_1(E,\Z)/H_1(E,\Z)^+$ --- the group on which the regulator is naturally
+defined.  Both sides being $\Q$-linear in the symbol, \eqref{eq:bloch}
+extends from $K_2(E)$ to $K_2(E)\otimes\Q$, hence to all tempered
+symbols.  For $\Delta>0$ the quotient and anti-invariant generators
+coincide (Lemma~\ref{lem:coinvariant}~(1)), and \eqref{eq:bloch} is then
+exactly the statement of \cite[Thm.~6]{LalinRam} as used in their
+conductor-$17$ computation; this is the instance our Appendix~\ref{app:k1}
+relies on.  For $\Delta<0$ the two readings differ by the index $2$
+(Lemma~\ref{lem:coinvariant}~(2)), and the quotient reading
+\eqref{eq:bloch} is confirmed on the present curve by three independent
+tempered symbols and by a direct numerical evaluation of $\int_{[b]}\eta$
+(Remark~\ref{rem:diamondk0}).
 
